<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mars (mythology)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mars was the Roman god of war and, in early times, also a guardian of farmland, second in importance only to Jupiter in the state religion. Regarded as the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and Remus, he was honoured with festivals in March, the month named after him, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Across the provinces he was identified with local gods, and the wolf and the woodpecker were sacred to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mars-mythology.jpg" alt="Mars (mythology)" width="320" height="559" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Statue_de_Mars_(Pyrrhus)_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_MC0058_(2).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
